{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775334009-FIPd5stZQDkhDP4flQMl","role":"assistant","created":1775334011,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at the page 12 figures, there are two figures on page 12 (elements 107 and 111). These are likely the charts for unemployment rate and payroll employment. Let me check the text resource file for the actual chart data, which may contain the values in a table format. Let me also look at what follows the \"Payroll Employment\" caption.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_4c0081874a3d4b689b1053be","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '350,370p' /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2007_09.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_39489263-5d48-4bca-9d28-e877ab13a006","role":"user","created":1775334011,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_4c0081874a3d4b689b1053be","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(Percent)\n\nPayroll Employment\n\n(Average monthly change in thousands\n\nfrom end of quarter to end of quarter\n\nSeptember 2007\n\n6\n\nPROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\n\nReal disposable personal income and consumer spending\n\nGrowth in personal income and outlays slowed in the second quarter of 2007 relative to the first, but nonetheless experienced solid gains. Nominal personal income rose 4.0 percent at an annual rate in the second quarter of 2007, following growth of 10.5 percent in the previous quarter. Wages and salaries, accounting for more than 50 percent of personal income, rose at a 2.8 percent annual rate in the second quarter, following a gain of 10.7 percent in the first quarter. The unusually large first-quarter gain was driven by bonuses and exercised stock options, which are counted as wage income in the national accounts.\n\nGrowth of personal income receipts on assets stayed strong with a 9.2 percent increase at an annual rate after a 10.5 percent rise during the first quarter. Growth of personal interest income increased at a 6.2 percent annual pace after rising 8.7 percent in the first quarter.\n\nReal disposable (after-tax) income declined 0.8 percent at an annual rate in the second quarter following two strong quarters of growth averaging more than 6 percent. Real personal consumption expenditures increased at a 1.3 percent annual pace, 2.4 percentage points slower than the first quarter growth rate. The personal saving rate (personal saving as a percentage of disposable personal income) dropped 0.5 percentage point to 0.6 percent in the second quarter from 1.1 percent in the first quarter. After the annual GDP revision released in July, the personal saving rate, which had been consistently negative for 2 years, was revised up. It now appears that personal disposable income was larger than or equal to consumer spending in every quarter since the third quarter of 2005.","annotations":{"priority":0.0}}],"structuredContent":{"stdout":"(Percent)\n\nPayroll Employment\n\n(Average monthly change in thousands\n\nfrom end of quarter to end of quarter\n\nSeptember 2007\n\n6\n\nPROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\n\nReal disposable personal income and consumer spending\n\nGrowth in personal income and outlays slowed in the second quarter of 2007 relative to the first, but nonetheless experienced solid gains. Nominal personal income rose 4.0 percent at an annual rate in the second quarter of 2007, following growth of 10.5 percent in the previous quarter. Wages and salaries, accounting for more than 50 percent of personal income, rose at a 2.8 percent annual rate in the second quarter, following a gain of 10.7 percent in the first quarter. The unusually large first-quarter gain was driven by bonuses and exercised stock options, which are counted as wage income in the national accounts.\n\nGrowth of personal income receipts on assets stayed strong with a 9.2 percent increase at an annual rate after a 10.5 percent rise during the first quarter. Growth of personal interest income increased at a 6.2 percent annual pace after rising 8.7 percent in the first quarter.\n\nReal disposable (after-tax) income declined 0.8 percent at an annual rate in the second quarter following two strong quarters of growth averaging more than 6 percent. Real personal consumption expenditures increased at a 1.3 percent annual pace, 2.4 percentage points slower than the first quarter growth rate. The personal saving rate (personal saving as a percentage of disposable personal income) dropped 0.5 percentage point to 0.6 percent in the second quarter from 1.1 percent in the first quarter. After the annual GDP revision released in July, the personal saving rate, which had been consistently negative for 2 years, was revised up. It now appears that personal disposable income was larger than or equal to consumer spending in every quarter since the third quarter of 2005.","stderr":"","exit_code":0},"isError":false}}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
